From faa8046b761f47c31b535254505e9ca9e1733df2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: emergent-agent-e1 <emergent-agent-e1@nivxray>
Date: Mon, 19 Oct 2026 17:57:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Re-enrolment must not move a computer out of its group
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Found while proving the LIVE backend compatible for a Windows endpoint
recovery, by driving the deployed enrolment code against a real database
instead of reading it: the DELIVERY record survives a re-enrolment (P0-3),
but FLEET PLACEMENT did not.

EndpointRecord carries group_id, policy_id, placement_basis, placement_at,
deployment_id, policy_source, policy_assigned_at and policy_assigned_by and
defaults them all to None. store.enroll $set the whole record, so every
re-enrolment CLEARED the placement — and the enrolment route then saw an
endpoint with no group and gave it the DEFAULT group and policy. A
reinstalled or recovered computer silently left the group an administrator
chose, and a stricter policy was replaced by the default one. The route's
own comment claimed the opposite; its guard was correct, but the write it
guarded against had already happened one layer down.

Measured: group_preserved=false, policy_preserved=false on a REVOKED ->
fresh token -> ACTIVE recovery.

Placement is an ADMINISTRATOR DECISION, not an enrolment fact, so it joins
the delivery record in the insert-only partition: a new endpoint is still
placed at enrolment, an existing one keeps what it was given. The two
placement helpers now return the EFFECTIVE placement rather than the
intended one, which on a re-enrolment named a group the endpoint was not in.

6 new tests cover identity continuity, placement and policy provenance, the
route's guard from both sides, an exhaustive partition check on
EndpointRecord so a future field must be classified deliberately, tenant
isolation, token single-use, the revoked credential and that a refused
recovery changes nothing. Two FAIL against the unfixed tree. 35 passed with
the Windows recovery and staging-race suites. No production data was read or
written. NOT deployed, NOT merged.
